2.3 Memory Injections COME BACK TO THIS WITH LESS DISTRACTIONS 

Batch 1 overview: Application and OS vulnerabilities

1. Memory injections (including buffer overflows)

When a program runs, it stores data in memory in neat, labelled boxes. A buffer overflow happens when a program puts more data into a box than it can hold. Think of pouring two litres into a one-litre jug: the extra spills into the box next door and overwrites whatever was there. An attacker carefully designs that spill so it contains their own instructions, and the computer then runs them.

Memory injection is the broader idea: sneaking malicious code into a running program’s memory so that a trusted program runs it. It’s like slipping a fake page into a recipe book while someone is in the middle of cooking from it.

2. Race conditions

This is a timing weakness: two things happen at nearly the same moment, and the system gets confused about the order. The term to remember is TOCTOU (time-of-check to time-of-use). Here’s a bank example: you have $100. You make two $100 withdrawals at exactly the same time. Both requests check the balance, both see $100, and both are approved before the balance updates. You get $200 out of a $100 account. The weakness is the gap between checking and using.

3. Malicious updates

You trust software updates because they come from the vendor. An attacker who compromises the vendor’s update system can slip malware into a legitimate-looking update. Everyone installs it because it looks completely normal. It’s like a trusted delivery driver bringing a parcel that someone tampered with at the depot. This links to supply chain, which you covered in 2.2. A famous real case is SolarWinds (2020).

4. Operating system vulnerabilities

Windows, macOS and Linux are huge and complex, so flaws are always being found. This is why Patch Tuesday exists: Microsoft releases fixes on the second Tuesday of each month. The danger is the gap between a patch being released and organisations actually installing it. Attackers study the patches to work out what was broken, then target anyone who hasn’t updated yet. Your rollout work at Medical Imaging Australia sits right in this space.

