Why would a company block a rooted phone from accessing work email? 
CompTIA Security+ 2.3: Mobile Device Vulnerabilities

Phones come with built-in guard rails. The official app store checks apps before you install them, and the operating system keeps each app in its own space so it can’t interfere with other apps or the system. Most mobile vulnerabilities come from removing those guard rails, either by accident or on purpose.

Sideloading means installing apps from outside the official app store. Nobody has checked these apps for malware. It’s like buying medicine from a stranger’s car boot instead of a pharmacy. I’ve seen this first-hand with someone I care for who isn’t confident with technology. He’d see an ad on Facebook promising money or an investment return, tap it, and land on a website warning that his phone “needed cleaning.” He’d tap install, more pop-ups would appear, he’d install more, and soon his phone was stuck in a loop he couldn’t escape. Some of this was probably sideloaded malware or adware. Some may have been browser notification spam, where a scam site tricks you into tapping “Allow” and then floods your phone with fake alerts. Two Android settings help prevent this: switch “Install unknown apps” off for browsers and Facebook, and remove any unrecognised sites from Chrome’s notification settings.

How I handled it. Twice, I did a full factory reset, because when you can’t be sure a device is clean, wiping it is the only safe option. In security terms, that’s remediation. After the first time, I set it up so nothing could be installed without my PIN. After the second time, I set up parental controls so every download needs my approval. That’s essentially a home version of MDM (Mobile Device Management), which businesses use to control what goes on work phones. In future when I get a new modem I will setup a guest WIFI so that it doesn't cause a problem for my network. This is segmentation, keeping evey part secure so it cant creep or pivot into my network.
Right now, everything shares one network, so any device on it can potentially “see” and reach every other device, including your laptop. A guest network gives a guest a key to the front gate only. He can get out to the street (the internet), but there’s no internal door into the house.

In a business, the idea is the same, just bigger. From Network+:

VLANs divide one physical network into separate virtual networks: staff, guests, printers, security cameras and so on.
Separate SSIDs are often linked to each VLAN. That’s why an office might have “Company-Staff” and “Company-Guest” Wi-Fi.
Firewall rules or ACLs between the segments control what’s allowed to cross. Guests usually get internet only, with no access to internal systems.

Many guest networks also have client isolation, which stops guest devices from even seeing each other. So if one guest’s phone is infected, it can’t reach another guest’s phone either. The main trade-off is that devices on the guest network usually can’t reach things on your main network, like a shared printer or a casting device

Jailbreaking (iPhone) and rooting (Android) are different. They’re deliberate. The owner breaks the phone’s built-in restrictions to get full control, so they can install anything and change system settings. But this also removes the protections that keep malware contained. It’s like taking out every internal door and lock in your house because they’re inconvenient. Installing updates often undoes a jailbreak, so people avoid updating, which leaves the phone unpatched and exposed. This is different from turning on Android’s Developer options, which are built in and don’t remove the phone’s security protections (though it’s wise to switch off USB debugging when you’re not using it).

Why businesses care. If an employee’s jailbroken or rooted phone has work email, files or apps on it, the risk spreads to the whole company, because an attacker could use that phone as a way back into the network. That’s why companies use MDM to detect and block these phones. I apply the same thinking at home: I don’t want a possibly compromised phone sharing my Wi-Fi with my own devices. Putting untrusted devices on a separate guest network is a simple form of network segmentation. My own modem is 8 to 10 years old and may not support it, which is a reminder that old hardware is a vulnerability too. Once a device stops receiving firmware updates, it’s end-of-life, and any new flaws will never be fixed.

Zero-day 2.3 
What “zero day” actually counts. It’s not quite “zero days since anyone knew about it.” It means the vendor has had zero days to fix it. An attacker might have known about the flaw and used it for months. It’s still a zero-day as long as the vendor doesn’t know, or knows but hasn’t released a patch yet.

Two related terms:

Zero-day vulnerability: the flaw itself
Zero-day exploit or attack: using that flaw before a fix exists

hy zero-days are so dangerous. This was the question from the overview, and you’ve partly answered it:

No patch exists, so you can’t fix it the normal way.
Antivirus often can’t detect it, because traditional antivirus looks for known threats, and this one is new.
How you defend against something you don’t know about. This part is worth knowing for the exam. You can’t patch it, so you rely on layers:

Behaviour-based detection (EDR): looks for suspicious activity rather than known malware
Least privilege and segmentation: limit how far an attacker can get once inside (your guest Wi-Fi thinking again)
Patch quickly once a fix is released
The sandbox examples Messer mentioned. A sandbox is a contained area where a browser or app runs code so that it can’t reach the rest of the system. Some of the most valuable zero-days are sandbox escapes: flaws that let malicious code break out of that container. It’s the same idea as VM escape, which you’ll see in the virtualisation video. A famous real-world example of zero-days in action is Stuxnet, which used several zero-days at once to sabotage Iran’s nuclear program. That’s nation-state work, which links back to 2.1.



CompTIA Security+ 2.3: Zero-day Vulnerabilities

A zero-day vulnerability is a hidden flaw in software that the vendor doesn’t know about yet, or knows about but hasn’t fixed. The name means the vendor has had zero days to fix it. An attacker might have known about the flaw and been using it for months, but as long as there’s no patch, it’s still a zero-day. Using the flaw before a fix exists is called a zero-day exploit or zero-day attack.

A zero-day is different from a misconfiguration. A misconfiguration is a weakness created by how something was set up, like a door left unlocked. A zero-day is a flaw in the code itself, like a lock with a manufacturing defect that nobody at the factory has noticed. It doesn’t matter how carefully you lock up.

Why are zero-days so dangerous? You can’t protect against something nobody knows exists. There’s no patch to install, and traditional antivirus often can’t detect the attack, because it looks for known threats. It becomes a race: will the vendor find and fix the flaw first, or will an attacker find it and use it? Zero-days are valuable, so they’re often used by well-funded attackers like nation-states. A famous example is Stuxnet, which used several zero-days at once to sabotage Iran’s nuclear program.

How do you defend against an unknown flaw? You use layers. Behaviour-based detection (EDR) looks for suspicious activity instead of known malware. Least privilege and network segmentation limit how far an attacker can get once inside. And when a patch is finally released, you install it quickly, because attackers study patches to work out what was broken.

Sandboxes. A sandbox is a contained space where code runs separately from the rest of the system, so if something goes wrong, the damage stays inside. Some of the most valuable zero-days are sandbox escapes, flaws that let malicious code break out of that container. I use this idea myself: I run Kali Linux in a virtual machine, so if anything goes wrong, I can delete the VM and my laptop is unaffected. Windows also includes Windows Sandbox (on Pro and Enterprise editions), a disposable copy of Windows running inside Windows. You can open a suspicious attachment there instead of on your main computer, and everything is wiped when you close it. One important point: a VM or sandbox protects your computer’s files, but it can still reach your network if networking is switched on. When testing something suspicious, switch networking off so malware can’t reach other devices on your Wi-Fi.

Operating System 2.3 
Overview: Operating System Vulnerabilities

The big idea: An operating system like Windows, macOS or Linux is enormous, with tens of millions of lines of code written by thousands of people. With that much code, mistakes are guaranteed. Some of those mistakes become security holes. And because the OS sits underneath everything on the computer, a flaw there can give an attacker control of the whole machine.

Think of the OS as the foundations and frame of a house. A faulty lock on one door is bad. A crack in the foundations affects every room.

Key points:

1. Flaws are found constantly
This isn’t a rare event. New OS vulnerabilities are discovered every month, by vendors, security researchers and attackers. That’s why every major OS has a regular update cycle.

2. Patch Tuesday
Microsoft releases its security fixes on the second Tuesday of each month. Each month’s release often fixes dozens of vulnerabilities at once, and some of them are rated critical, meaning an attacker could take control of the computer remotely.

3. The danger window
Once a patch is released, attackers study it to work out what was broken, then go after everyone who hasn’t installed it yet. So the period after a patch comes out, before organisations install it, is when unpatched systems are most at risk. Sometimes this is described as “Patch Tuesday, Exploit Wednesday.”

4. Best practices for patching BACK UP YOUR DATA Having a OS backup image using Rufus like for my Ubuntu server is a good idea. 

Patch regularly and promptly. Don’t let updates pile up
.
Test first. In a business, updates are tested on a few machines before rolling out to everyone, because a patch can occasionally break an important application.
Have a fallback plan. Take backups and know how to roll back an update if it causes problems.
Automate where possible. Tools like WSUS, Intune or other patch management systems push updates to many computers at once.
Why operating systems are a big target?
find one flaw, and you’ve found it for millions of machines. That scale is exactly what makes OS flaws so valuable. One working exploit can be used against every unpatched computer running that OS.

The risk after a patch?
Attackers reverse-engineer the patch to work out what was broken.
People delay updates. A day becomes a week, and that’s a week of exposure. “Patch Tuesday, Exploit Wednesday” is exactly right.

Why test before rolling out?
one bad patch pushed to everyone at once could take down mortgages, cards, the whole bank.

Pilot group or phased rollout: patch a small group first, check nothing breaks, then expand.
Test environment: ideally, patches are tested on non-production machines before going anywhere near real users.

The pivoting point needs separating out. Two different risks got blended together:
Rollout risk: a bad patch breaks a whole clinic at once. Doing one or two machines first reduces this.
Exposure risk: any machine still waiting for the patch is vulnerable, and an attacker could pivot between them.
Every phased rollout has this trade-off: going slowly is safer for stability, but leaves machines exposed for longer. Businesses balance it by patching the most critical and internet-facing systems first, and by segmenting networks, like keeping clinics separate, so one compromised machine can’t easily reach the rest.

Overview: Malicious Updates 2.3

The big idea: We’re always told to install updates, and for good reason. But what if the update itself is the attack? A malicious update is malware delivered through a legitimate update channel. Because it comes from a trusted source, it looks normal, passes the usual checks, and gets installed by everyone.

It’s like a trusted delivery company bringing you a parcel that someone tampered with at the depot. You’d never suspect it, because you know the driver.

How it happens:

Compromising the vendor: attackers break into the software company’s systems, often the servers that build or distribute updates, and slip their code into a genuine update before it’s released.
Real digital signatures: because the update comes out of the vendor’s own system, it’s signed by the real vendor. The signature proves who it came from, but not that the vendor’s systems were clean. This links back to digital signatures in 1.4.
Fake update prompts: the less sophisticated version is a pop-up like “Your browser is out of date, click to update” on a dodgy website. That’s closer to what happened with Al’s phone.

A famous example: SolarWinds (2020).
Attackers broke into SolarWinds, whose network monitoring software is used by thousands of organisations, including US government agencies. They hid a backdoor inside a genuine software update. Around 18,000 customers installed it, and it went undetected for months. It was attributed to a nation-state, which links back to 2.1. It’s also a supply chain attack, which links to 2.2 and the next video.

How to defend against it:

Only download updates from the official source, never from pop-ups or links.
Test updates on a small group before rolling them out widely (your phased rollout point from the OS topic).
Monitor for unusual behaviour after updates, since a valid signature isn’t proof of safety.
Vendors must protect their own build systems, because they’re a prime target.

The tension to notice: you’re told to patch quickly, but a patch could be the attack. That’s why testing and monitoring matter, not just speed.

Why is a malicious update so hard to spot? If an update is properly digitally signed, why isn’t that enough to prove it’s safe?
Q1: Why are malicious updates so hard to spot?
Ebay has been doing updates for their app for ages and fortunatly never had an issue.  It comes through the official app, from a company people have trusted with their money for years. Even a tech-savvy person has no way to tell. Its a catch-22 perfectly: don’t update and you’re exposed to known flaws; update and the update itself could be the attack.

How the catch-22 is actually handled

I could propose wait a day for personal devices? 
For businesses: that’s the phased rollout from the OS topic. Patch a small pilot group first, watch for problems, then expand. A day or two of waiting catches both broken patches and malicious ones. The exception is a critical patch for a flaw that’s already being exploited. That gets fast-tracked, because the known danger outweighs the small chance of a bad update.
For individuals: keep automatic updates on, from official sources. Malicious updates like SolarWinds are rare. Unpatched devices being attacked through known flaws is far more common. So on balance, updating is still the safer choice.
Afterwards: your instinct to monitor is exactly right. Businesses use behaviour-based tools (EDR) that flag unusual activity, like a trusted program suddenly contacting a strange server. That’s how SolarWinds was eventually caught.

Q2: Why isn’t a digital signature enough? 

Think back to 1.4. A digital signature proves two things:

Who it came from (the real vendor)
That it hasn’t been changed since it was signed

What it can’t prove is that the code was clean at the moment it was signed. In SolarWinds, the attackers inserted their code before the signing step. So the vendor’s own system signed the malware, and the signature was completely genuine.

Think of tamper-evident packaging. The seal on a medicine bottle proves nobody opened it after it left the factory. But if someone contaminated the medicine on the factory line, before the seal went on, the seal is intact and genuine, and it tells you nothing. The signature is the seal. The attack happened inside the factory.

