Supply Chain 2.3

Overview: Supply Chain Vulnerabilities

The big idea: No organisation builds everything itself. It relies on other companies for services, hardware and software. Every one of those suppliers is a potential way in. Your security is only as strong as the weakest company you trust.

Think of your house again. You might have excellent locks, but you’ve given a key to the cleaner, the plumber and the alarm company. If any of them is careless with that key, or one of their staff is dishonest, your locks don’t matter.

Messer breaks it into three types:

1. Service providers
Companies with access to your systems: IT support (MSPs), cloud providers, contractors, maintenance companies. Attackers target them because one breach can open doors into all their clients.

Example: you already know this one. In the Target breach (2013), attackers got in using login details stolen from Target’s air-conditioning contractor, then moved from there into the payment systems.
Australian example: the Medibank breach (2022) reportedly began with stolen login details belonging to a worker at a third-party IT provider.

2. Hardware providers
Can you trust that the equipment is genuine and hasn’t been tampered with?

Counterfeit hardware sold cheaply can contain hidden flaws or backdoors. There have been cases of fake network switches made to look like a well-known brand, sold through unofficial sellers.
Defence: buy from the manufacturer or authorised resellers, not the cheapest listing online.

3. Software providers
Every app and library you install is code someone else wrote.

Example: SolarWinds, which you just covered. A malicious update is a software supply chain attack.
Modern software is built from open-source libraries, so one flaw in a widely used library can affect thousands of products at once.

How organisations defend against it:

Vet suppliers before signing. Check their security practices, and write security requirements into the contract.
Least privilege for vendors. Give suppliers access only to what they need, only when they need it. Target’s air-conditioning contractor never needed a path to payment systems.
Segmentation. Keep vendor access in its own section of the network.

Monitor vendor activity, and remove access as soon as a contract ends. That’s what I did at Orica everyday.


